<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Whether this device is free to rotate outside the player.

      False on a phone, and that is a statement about the *pages*, not about the
      player: the home feed, the library grid, the frosted bars and the floating
      tab bar are all laid out for a tall single column, and a phone turned
      sideways gets a worse version of every one of them.

      The player is the exception and is handled where it belongs — while it is
      up, MainActivity lets the window follow the user's own rotation setting
      into the same two-column player a tablet gets, and pins it upright again
      when the player closes. A screen too small for that shape stays pinned
      rather than squashing the portrait player.

      The sw600dp copy of this file overrides it to true — 600dp on the shortest
      side is the line Android itself draws for "this is not a phone", and past
      it a tablet held in landscape is the ordinary way to hold one rather than
      a phone being turned on its side for a moment. MainActivity reads it once,
      at startup.
    -->
    <bool name="allow_rotation">false</bool>
</resources>
